<spring:beans xmlns="http://membrane-soa.org/proxies/1/"
              xmlns:spring="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans"
              xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
              xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans
                    http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans/spring-beans-4.2.xsd
                    http://membrane-soa.org/proxies/1/ http://membrane-soa.org/schemas/proxies-1.xsd">

    <!-- Scenario 4: same topology/body/backlog as loadtest-fullproxy.xml, but every request must
         first pass HTTP Basic credential verification and then a per-client rate-limit check
         before being forwarded, real gateway tasks a plain reverse proxy cannot do. This
         measures the compounded cost of stacking both checks on one request, the way a
         production API would; run rateLimiter and basicAuthentication as separate scenarios
         instead if you need each one's individual cost isolated.

         basicAuthentication runs first (authenticating the request before rate-limiting it, the
         common production order), then rateLimiter. basicAuthentication uses the built-in static
         user list (StaticUserDataProvider) rather than htpasswd/JDBC/LDAP, so there is no backing
         store I/O and no deliberately slow hash, but it is not just a byte compare: every request
         pays for a Base64 decode, several String/substring allocations for the credentials, a
         HashMap allocation to bridge into the generic UserDataProvider.verify(Map) interface, two
         more HashMap lookups, and up to four regex Matcher allocations in
         SecurityUtils.matchesHashPattern/verifyPassword, sniffing whether the incoming and stored
         passwords look like a bcrypt/crypt(3) hash (they never do here) before falling through to
         the actual MessageDigest.isEqual compare, plus a BasicHttpSecurityScheme and a List.of
         allocation on success. removeAuthorizationHeader is left at its default (true).
         rateLimiter's requestLimit is set far above this test's total request count (up to
         11,000,000 with the default 1,000,000 warmup at LOAD_TOTAL=10000000) so no request is ever actually rejected with 429; default keying (client IP)
         is used, and since all load comes from one client VM, every request hits the same counter
         key, the worst case for the interceptor's internal lock contention (LazyRateLimit: a
         ConcurrentHashMap increment plus a synchronized clock check on every single request, with
         no comparable per-request allocation). The gateway is already CPU-saturated in every
         scenario here (roughly 95% busy), so basicAuthentication's extra allocation/regex work
         shows up directly as lost throughput (measured around 5% against fullproxy), while
         rateLimiter's near-allocation-free path measures as noise (under 1%).

         run-scenario.sh sends valid credentials for the "loadtest" user on every request, so
         every request passes both checks and is forwarded; none are rejected with 401 or 429. A
         run reporting ERR!=0 here is invalid, not just noisy, since either short-circuit is
         cheaper than a proxied request and would inflate RPS rather than deflate it.
         __BACKEND_PRIVATE_IP__ is filled in by deploy-and-run.sh once the backend VM's private IP
         is known. -->
    <!-- No exchange store: the default LimitedMemoryExchangeStore keeps recent exchanges for the
         admin console and serializes request threads on its lock; nothing here reads them. -->
    <forgetfulExchangeStore id="forgetful"/>

    <router exchangeStore="forgetful">
        <transport backlog="1024"/>
        <api port="2000">
            <basicAuthentication>
                <user name="loadtest" password="loadtest-secret"/>
            </basicAuthentication>
            <rateLimiter requestLimit="100000000" requestLimitDuration="PT1H"/>
            <target host="__BACKEND_PRIVATE_IP__" port="2010"/>
        </api>
    </router>
</spring:beans>
